Glucose oxidase (GOx), traditionally regarded as an oxidoreductase with high $\upbeta$ -D-glucose specificity, has been widely applied as sensing probe for $\upbeta$ -D-glucose detection. However, it is found that the specificity of $\mathbf{GOx}$ is not absolute and GOx cannot decern $\upbeta$ -D-glucose among its isomers such as xylose, mannose and galactose. The existence of the other monosaccharides in sensing system could compromise the sensitivity for $\upbeta$ -D-glucose, therefore, it is of great urgency to achieve the highly specific catalytic performance of GOx. Herein, porous metalorganic frameworks (MOF) are prepared as the host matrix for immobilization of both GOx and bovine hemo­ globin (BHb), obtained a cascaded catalytic system $\left(\mathbf{MOF@GOx@BHb}\right)$ with both enhanced GOx activity and peroxidase-like activity owing to the spatially confined effect. Then, using $\upbeta$ -D-glucose as both template mole­ cules and substances, hydroxyl radicals are produced continuously and applied for initiating the polymerization of molecular imprinting polymers (MIPs) on the surface of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ . Impressively, the obtaining molecularly imprinted GOx (noted as $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb-MIPs}\operatorname{J}$ achieves the highly sensitive and specific detec­ tion of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose in the concentration range of $0.5\mathrm{-}20~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with the $\mathrm{LOD}=0.4\upmu\mathrm{M}$ $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ ) by colorimetry. Similarly, MOF@GOx $@$ BHb-MIPs are subsequently obtained using mannose, xylose and galactose as template molecules, respectively, and also show satisfied specific catalytic activity towards corresponding templates, indicating the effectiveness of the proposed strategy to achieve highly specific catalytic performance of GOx.
## 1. Introduction
Glucose oxidase (GOx), formed by a dimeric glycoprotein with two non-covalently bound flavin adenine dinucleotides (FAD) coenzymes, is usually regarded as a kind of oxido-reductase which could specifically and efficiently catalyze $\upbeta$ -D-glucose into hydrogen peroxide and glu­ conic acid with the help of molecular oxygen as electron acceptors (Chen, T. et al., 2020; Fu et al., 2018). Consequently, GOx has been widely applied in biosensors fabrication as sensing probe for glucose detection by both colorimetry and electrochemistry(Luong et al., 2017; Mano, 2019). Nevertheless, few researches focused on the study of the specific catalytic performance of GOx towards the isomers of $\upbeta$ -D-glu­ cose. Surprisingly, it was accidently found that GOx could catalyze a series of monosaccharides such as $\upbeta$ -D-glucose, xylose, mannose and galactose in our work. In other words, GOx is truly lack of specificity and cannot solely catalyze $\upbeta$ -D-glucose among its isomers. Therefore, the existence of the other monosaccharides in the sensing system could interfere the accurate sensing of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose. Therefore, it is of great importance and urgency to achieve the highly specific catalysis or detection of GOx.
Molecular imprinting technology (MIT) has rapidly gained enormous interest due to its high effectiveness in specific recognition such as extraction(Stevenson et al., 2016), separation(Rutkowska et al., 2018) and chemical sensing(Tiwari and Prasad, 2015; Yang et al., 2018). Thus, it could be anticipated that the introduction of molecular imprinting polymers (MIPs)(Lu et al., 2019a, 2019b; Yang et al., 2018) would contribute to enhancing the specific catalytic performance of GOx by building artificial recognition sites with special 3D structures on GOx surface using $\upbeta$ -D-glucose as template(Lin et al., 2018). However, high reaction temperature could be inevitable in the synthesis of MIPs, which could cause the deactivation of fragile GOx(Huang et al., 2017; Ranji-­ Burachaloo et al., 2019). Besides, initiators or catalysts with compli­ cated structures such as AIBN and $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}$ are also essential for the polymerization of monomers and crosslinkers while they could increase the complexity of MIPs purification in general(Cai et al., 2019; Mathew et al., 2018). Therefore, facile and mild systems for polymerization are urgently required for the construction of MIPs in the presence of fragile and flexible GOx. Hydroxyl radicals generated from hydrogen peroxide could initiate the synthesis of diverse polymers such as polyacrylic acid (Liu et al., 2020), poly $(N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylacrylamide)(Reyhani et al., 2018) at room temperature, which can be an appropriate method for the preparation of MIPs on the GOx surface. It should be noted that hydrogen peroxide will be generated during the catalysis process of glucose by GOx. Therefore, a cascaded enzymatic system based on the combination of GOx and peroxidase will be beneficial for hydroxyl radical generation, which could provide a mild working condition for MIPs preparation in the presence of $\mathbf{GOx}$ (Attieh et al., 2017; Bossi and Haupt, 2020; Chen, T. et al., 2020).
Bovine hemoglobin (BHb), as a kind of protein with peroxidase-like activity, has been applied for the construction of cascaded enzymatic system with GOx(Li et al., 2019; Yu et al., 2018). However, macromo­ lecular enzymes generally tend to self-aggregate in solution which could result in the obvious activity loss due to the concealed active sites by each other(Chen et al., 2018). Therefore, a well-dispersed and porous framework for GOx and BHb immobilization is also urgently required for the construction of a cascaded enzymatic system with enhanced cata­ lytic activity. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a kind of porous crystalline hybrid materials with tunable pore sizes and volumes, have attracted great attention as host matrixes for wide applications in gas storage or separation(Li et al., 2020), catalysis(Lu et al., 2020) and sensors(Koo et al., 2019), etc. Besides, MOFs could provide a spatially confined space with proximity effect for enzyme immobilization to in­ crease local enzyme concentration and avoid the leakage of intermediate products, which could further enhance the chance of substrates to access the enzyme active sites and thus obviously increase the catalytic effi­ ciency of the constructed cascaded enzymatic system(Kuc et al., 2020; Zeng et al., 2015). Therefore, it is strongly desired that the porous MOFs could be applied as an ideal host matrix for the immobilization of GOx and BHB, which could create a stable microenvironment for enzyme through specific host-guest interactions(Gkaniatsou et al., 2016).
Herein, porous MOFs were chosen as the host matrix for immobili­ zation of both GOx and BHb, obtaining a cascaded catalytic system $\left(\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}\right)$ with both enhanced GOx activity and peroxidaselike activity. In the presence of excessive $\upbeta$ -D-glucose, hydroxyl radi­ cals could be generated continuously in the confined enzymatic systems and applied for initiating the polymerization of MIPs on the surface of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ . The obtaining molecularly imprinted GOx (noted as $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb-MIPs}\operatorname{\delta}$ achieved the sensitive and specific detection of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose. With the similar method, $\mathsf{M O F@G O x@B H b–M I P s–m}$ , $\mathrm{MOF@GOx@BHb–MIPs–X}$ and $\mathrm{MOF@GOx@BHb–MIPs-g}$ were also suc­ cessfully obtained using mannose, xylose and galactose as template respectively and showed improved specific catalytic activity towards related template, which indicated the effectiveness of the proposed strategy to achieve the highly specific catalytic performance of GOx.
