## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fabrication and characterization of MOF, MOF@GOx, MOF@BHb and MOF@GOx@BHb
As shown in Fig. 1, the nano-caged enzymes including MOF@GOx, MOF@BHb, MOF@GOx@BHb, and MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs were pre­ pared by co-precipitation of enzyme with MOF. Then, their morphologies as well as element distributions were characterized by SEM and TEM, respectively. Moreover, the pure MOF was also prepared and characterized as the control. As shown in Fig. 2A, the SEM images indicated that the as-synthesized pure MOF was in dodecahedron-like shape with the average size of $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Then, upon the addition of enzyme in the preparation process of MOF, the obtained MOF@GOx (Figure S3A), MOF@BHb (Figure S3B) and MOF@GOx@BHb (Fig. 2B) still retained the polyhedral structure, which was further confirmed by the TEM images (Fig. 2G and H). In a word, the obtained composite samples showed slight difference in morphology but still remained the primary structure of the dodecahedron-like MOF even after the intro­ duction of different guests into the MOF hosts, which confirmed the successful construction of the nano-caged nanoreactors. Simultaneously, the successful construction of the nanoreactors was also characterized by SEM element mapping. As shown in Fig. 2D, the obtained MOF was made up of C, O, N and Zn elements while the mass fraction of $Z\mathrm{n}$ element reached $6.95\%$ . After the introduction of GOx and BHb, Fe element was observed in the SEM element mapping image of the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ (Fig. 2E), proving that the enzyme was truly incor­ porated with MOF to form the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ composite.
For comparison, further analyses for the pure MOF as well as
MOF@GOx@BHb were carried out by FTIR as shown in Figure S4. Compared with the pure MOF, characteristic peaks of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ in FTIR spectrum at 1384, $1543~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were observed, which should be attributed to amide I band of the enzymes(Yang et al., 2020), confirming the successfully introduction of GOx and BHb into the MOF. After that, the crystal structure of pure MOF and the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ were both characterized by XRD and the related results are shown in Figure S5. It can be observed that the XRD patterns of the pure MOF (Figure S5A) matched well with that reported previously(Ren et al., 2019). Moreover, the XRD pattern of the pure MOF was similar to that of $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ ${\bf G}{\bf O}{\bf x}@{\bf B}{\bf H}{\bf b}$ (Figure S5B), indicating the crystal structure of pure MOF was not changed after the introduction of the enzymes (GOx and BHb). Afterwards, the XRD spectrum of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ (Figure S5C) after incubation in ABS was obtained to characterize its structural stability. It could be observed that the XRD spectrum of the incubated
MOF@GOx@BHb in ABS was similar to that of the pristine $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ , indicating that $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ could maintain its struc­ tural stability in the following preparation process of MIPs and catalytic activity test.
As illustrated in Fig. 1D, MIPs were prepared on the $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ ${\bf G}{\bf O}{\bf x}@{\bf B}{\bf H}{\bf b}$ surface (Fig. 1E) and the obtained $\mathsf{M O F@G O x@B H b–M I P s}$ were characterized by SEM, element mapping, TEM, FTIR XRD and BET, respectively. As shown in Fig. 2C, F and 2I, no obvious change in morphology could be observed in comparison with the $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ . However, a great amount of B element $(4.68\%)$ was observed in the SEM element mapping images, indicating that the sur­ face of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ was truly covered with the MIPs. Moreover, as shown in Figure S4, the characteristic absorption peak at $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ should be attributed to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{B}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ groups of the MIPs, indicating the suc­ cessful preparation of MIPs on the surface of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ . Besides, as shown in Figure S5D, except for the crystal structure of pure MOF (Figure S5A), the obvious “bread peaks”, which are usually regarded as the characterization peak of the amorphous polymer material, could be observed on the XRD patterns of $\operatorname{MOF@GOx@BHb-MIP}$ , proving the successful preparation of MIPs on the surface of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ and obtaining the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}-$ MIP subsequently. What’s more, no obvious spectrum of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose $(1100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}).$ ) could be observed in the FT-IR spectrum of MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs (Figure S4), indicating that the template $\mathrm{.}\mathrm{|}\mathfrak{\mathrm{\Gamma}}\mathrm{|}$ -D-glucose) has been successful removed from the poly­ mer matrix during extraction step. Besides, the pore diameters of MOF, $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ and $\operatorname{MOF@GOx@BHb-MIP}$ were characterized by BET analysis and the results are shown in Figure S6. It could be observed that after the encapsulation of enzyme in MOF, the pore size of MOF was decreased obviously. Moreover, the MIPs on the MOFs surface could further reduce the pore size of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ .
## 3.2. Catalytic activity tests for MOF, MOF@GOx, MOF@BHb and MOF@GOx@BHb
The special function of MOF in the as-prepared nanoreactors was systematically studied. Firstly, the influence of MOF on the peroxidaselike catalytic activity of BHb was studied and the results can be seen in Fig. 3A. No color change and obvious peroxidase-like catalytic activity could be observed from the lower inset and blue line of Fig. 3A, indi­ cating that the pure MOF had no catalytic activity. Afterwards, the catalytic activity of BHb decreased slightly upon the addition of a certain amount of MOF into the BHb solution, which proved that the physical mixing of MOF with BHb could not enhance the peroxidase-like catalytic activity of BHb. Likewise, as shown in Fig. 3B, the pure MOF did not display any GOx catalytic activity and could not increase the catalytic activity of GOx either.
For the construction of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ , it could be speculated that the cascade catalytic activity of nanoreactors MOF@GOx@BHb could be adjusted by regulating the feeding ratio of GOx/BHb (w/w) in the coprecipitation process. Therefore, the relative activity of $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ prepared with different feeding ratios was studied firstly. It can be seen from Figure S7, the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ with the feeding ratio (1:2) showed the highest catalytic activity and was accordingly chosen as the optimal sample for the following experiments.
Then, the effectiveness of porous MOF as framework in catalytic activity enhancement was investigated and the results can be seen in Fig. 3C and D. Firstly of all, peroxidase-like activities of BHb, $\mathbf{MOF@BHb}$ and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ were assayed (Fig. 3C). It could be found that the catalytic activity of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ was similar to that of $\mathbf{MOF@BHb}$ , which was obviously lower than that of pure BHb at the same mass concentration. This was mainly due to the much lower content of BHb in the obtained MOF@BHb and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ in comparation with the pure BHb. What’s more, the catalytic activity of the MOF@BHb with different mass concentration were also studied. It could be observed that the catalytic activity of MOF@BHb increased with the increasing mass concentration in the range of $0.5{-}2.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . Therein, the catalytic activity of MOF $@$ BHb exceeded that of BHb ( $\mathrm{0.5\mg/mL}$ when its concentration was more than $1.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . In our opinion, the reasons for catalytic invigorating effect by MOF should be as follows. Firstly, the design of the BHb encapsulated in the pre-designed cage of MOF could effectively avoids the enzyme aggregation. More importantly, the porous MOF could provide a powerful platform for the immobilization of BHb, in which a spatially confined space with proximity effect could be constructed and increase the probability of the substrates to access the active sites of BHb (Fig. 3E). Accordingly, the catalytic activity of BHb was obviously increased with the help of the MOF skeleton.
Then, the cascaded catalytic activities of pristine GOx, MOF@GOx and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ were measured. As shown in Fig. 3D, the cascaded catalytic activity of MOF@GOx was lower than that of GOx in the same mass concentration due to the low mass ratio of GOx in $\mathbf{MOF@GOx}$ . Interestingly, the catalytic activity of the obtained $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ was obviously higher than that of $\mathbf{MOF@GOx}$ and GOx in the same mass concentration. This was mainly because the MOF loaded with the cascaded enzyme (GOx and BHb) could obviously augment the local enzyme concentrations and avoid the leakage of in­ termediate products $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ produced from the catalysis of GOx, thus increasing the possibility of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reaching BHb active sites and simul­ taneously achieving the high catalytic efficiency of the cascaded enzy­ matic system. The diffusion of substrate and intermediate has a very low barrier and short path, which facilitate the conversion of substrates (Fig. 3E).
To obtain the kinetic parameters of the dual functional $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ with both peroxidase-like activity and GOx activity, the catalytic activity of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ was analyzed based on the enzyme kinetic theory. The initial rates of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ in the presence of TMB, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\upbeta$ -D-glucose at different concentrations were calculated at room temperature. Then, the initial rates and the recip­ rocal of the initial rate were plotted against different substrate concen­ trations and reciprocals of the substrate concentration using MichaelisMenten curves and Lineweaver Burk curves as shown in Fig. 4. The Vmax and the Km of the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ were calculated from a Lineweaver-Burk plot: $1/\mathbf{v}=\mathrm{Km/Vmax}(1/[\mathsf{S}]+1/\mathrm{Km})$ and the detailed values are shown in Table S1. It could be seen that the reciprocal of the initial rate increased linearly with the enhanced reciprocal of the sub­ strate concentration. The Km values of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ with TMB substrate were significantly lower than those of other peroxidase mimics (Ding et al., 2016). In addition, the Vmax value of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ using TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as a substrate was obviously larger than that of HRP. This suggested that $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ showed stronger affinity to both TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and possessed higher peroxidase-like activity to­ wards the catalytic oxidation of TMB than HRP, which resulted from the more active catalytic sites and larger specific surface area of the porous $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ .
The apparent substrate affinity $\mathbf{\Omega}^{\mathrm{Km}})$ and maximum conversion rate (Vmax) of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ proved the well-maintained catalytic ac­ tivities of the encapsulated BHb in MOF. Besides, the parameters of Km and Vmax for $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ toward catalysis of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose were calculated to be $0.541~\mathrm{mM}$ and $2.54\times10^{-8}\mathrm{M}/s$ indicating the con­ structed $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ owned the obviously higher cascaded cata­ lytic activity towards $\upbeta$ -D-glucose in comparison with other GOx-based cascade systems(Li et al., 2019; Sengupta et al., 2020). This supports that $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ could effectively react with $\upbeta$ -D-glucose in a proper concentration to generate enough hydroxyl radicals to initiate the polymerization for MIPs preparation.
From the viewpoint of practical usage, the stability and reusability are the key features for the enzymes to be considered. Therefore, the catalytic activities of the pristine GOx and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ in diverse working conditions including different pH values and temperature were characterized and the results are shown in Fig. 5A and B. It can be observed that both GOx and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ showed higher catalytic activity in acid condition, therefore, ABS $\mathrm{(pH}=5$ ) was chosen as the optimal solution in the following study. Moreover, the activity of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ increased with the rising temperature till $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ based on the fact that the structures of enzyme protein are relative stable at lower temperature, and the higher temperature could effectively accelerate the enzymatic reactions. However, when the temperature exceeded $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , irreversible structure change of the enzyme would happen, which could result in the loss of enzymatic activity. Interest­ ingly, the catalytic activity of $\operatorname{MOF@GOx@BHb}$ decreased more slowly in comparison to the pristine GOx, indicating the higher temperature tolerance of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ due to the introduction of MOF frame­ work. Then, the reusability of the $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ was also studied and the results can be seen in Fig. 5C. It was found that the catalytic activity of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ slightly dropped after five repeated tests, whereas that of the pure GOx decreased sharply. All in all, $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ exhibited obviously enhanced stability and reusability compared with the pristine GOx, which could be qualified as cascaded catalytic platform for the construction of MIPs on their surface.
3.3. Selectivity tests for pristine GOx and MOF@GOx@BHb for catalysis of diverse monosaccharides
The selectivity of GOx and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ in the presence of diverse monosaccharides were characterized and the results are shown in Fig. 5D and E. It can be observed that both GOx and $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ possessed higher catalytic activity towards $\upbeta$ -D-glucose. Moreover, obvious catalytic activity was still observed during the catalysis of mannose and xylose, respectively. Whereas, the $G0\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ showed much lower catalytic activity towards galactose $(5\%)$ . Therefore, it can be concluded that diverse monosaccharides including mannose, xylose and galactose could be catalyzed by GOx. In other words, the presence of mannose, xylose, and galactose in $\upbeta$ -Dglucose solution could interfere the sensing results of the GOx, which can be confirmed by the interference experiment as shown in Fig. 5F.
## 3.4. Construction of molecularly imprinted nanoreactors based on the cascaded catalytic system
As shown in Fig. 6A, in the presence of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose and oxygen, a large number of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can be generated by the catalysis of GOx in $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ and will be further transformed into hydroxyl radicals by the catalysis of BHb with peroxidase-like activity. Then, the gener­ ated hydroxyl radicals will initiate the crosslinked polymerization of boronic acid derivative in the presence of crosslinker and template. Accordingly, the crosslinked polymer was obtained and stabilized on the surface of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ . After the template removal, molecularly imprinted $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ with specific recognition ability $(\mathrm{MOF}@\$ GOx@BHb-MIPs, Fig. 6A) was finally obtained.
## 3.5. The performance characterization of the biosensors using MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs as sensing probes
After the molecular imprinting of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ , the cascaded catalytic activity of MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs was tested first and the re­ sults are shown in Fig. 6B. It can be found that the relative catalytic activity of MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs was lower than that of $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ ­ GOx@BHb in the same mass concentration $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL})$ , but still higher than that of the pristine GOx. Thus, the as-prepared $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}.$ MIPs still showed obvious catalytic activity towards $\upbeta$ -D-glucose and could be utilized as sensing probes for the detection of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose by colorimetry. Accordingly, the absorption spectra of the sensing system containing TMB, $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}.$ -MIPs and different concentrations of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose $(0.4{-}30~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ were recorded under the optimal conditions $\mathrm{(pH}=5\mathrm{)}$ ). As shown in Fig. 6C, the absorbance intensity at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ increased with the increasing $\upbeta$ -D-glucose concentration within the scope of the investigated concentration $(0.4{-}30~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . Firstly, the grad­ ually increased absorbance proved that the colorimetric assay could be applied for the detection of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose. Then, an obviously positive linear relationship could be observed between the $\upbeta$ -D-glucose concen­ tration and absorbance intensity in the range of $0.5\mathrm{-}20~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , while the regression equation of this linear relationship was fit as Absorbance $\l=$ $0.0686\mathrm{~+~}0.00398[\upbeta$ -D-glucose] with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{R}}^{2}=0.9955$ . What’s more, the LOD $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ for $\upbeta$ -D-glucose was calculated to be $0.4~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ , which was comparable to those of other GOx-based biosensors via colorimetric assay as summarized in Table S2.
The specific recognition capability of the $\mathsf{M O F@G O x@B H b–M I P s}$ and MOF@GOx@BHb-NIPs were further studied and the results are shown in Fig. 6D and Figure S8, respectively. It could be observed from Fig. 6D that the absorbance intensity of the solution containing $\upbeta$ -Dglucose was much higher than those of other isomers. Moreover, the relative selectivity coefficients of the pristine GOx, MOF@GOx@BHb, MOF@GOx@BHb-NIPs and MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs were calculated and the results are shown in Table S3. It could be observed that the relative selectivity coefficients of the molecularly imprinted sensing platform towards diverse interfering monosaccharides increased signif­ icantly compared to those of the pristine glucose oxidase, $\mathrm{MOF}@ $ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ , and MOF@GOx@BHb-NIPs, respectively, indicating that the constructed sensing system showed greatly enhanced specificity. This was mainly because xylose, mannose and galactose could hardly get through the MIPs due to the unmatched morphology between these monosaccharides and imprinted cavity (insert of Fig. 6D), while the $\upbeta$ -Dglucose could easily get through the barriers set up by MIPs and access the surface of GOx. For the MOF@GOx@BHb-NIPs (Figure S8), similar selectivity was observed compared with the pristine GOx and $\mathrm{MOF}@ $ ­ $\mathbf{GOx}@\mathbf{BHb}$ , which indicated the effectiveness of the MIPs in specific $\upbeta$ -Dglucose recognition. Besides, the potential influence of the other monosaccharides on the sensing results of the biosensors towards $\upbeta$ -Dglucose were also investigated and the results are shown in Fig. 6E. In detail, the absorbance intensities of the biosensors at $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ were recorded in the solutions containing $\upbeta$ -D-glucose and other mono­ saccharides. It could be observed that both the single isomer and the mixture of isomers of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose have slight influence on the sensing results of the biosensors, further indicating that the as-prepared bio­ sensors based on the MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs could discern $\upbeta$ -D-glucose among its isomers, largely avoiding the influence of other mono­ saccharides on the sensing results and ensuring the accuracy of the sensing results. Compared with other molecularly imprinted sensors using nanomaterials as sensing probes via electrochemistry (Table S4) (Chen, T. et al., 2020; Chen, Z.Y. et al., 2020; Cho et al., 2018; da Silva et al., 2021; Sehit et al., 2020; Uygun and Uygun, 2020; Zheng et al., 2018), our biosensor showed comparable or superior specific recogni­ tion ability, indicating that the MIPs could achieve the specific selec­ tivity of both enzyme and nano-catalysts.
## 3.6. The universality of the MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs prepared using other monosaccharides as templates
To study the universality of our proposed strategy, the MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs constructed using other monosaccharides including xylose, mannose and galactose as templates were prepared. Their selectivity was also tested and the results can be seen in Figure S9. It could be observed that the MOF@GOx@BHb-MIPs always showed the highest catalytic activity towards corresponding template molecules, which proves that the boracic monomer-based MIPs could effectively enhance the specificity of GOx. In detail, as shown in Figure S10A, MIPs were prepared based on the boracic monomers. After the template removal, MIPs with specific recognition sites were successfully con­ structed as shown in Figure S10B. During the recognition process, the molecules with the same structure to the template could easily get through the MIPs, while others are prohibited by the barriers set by MIPs. Therefore, all these results indicated that the strategy we supposed showed great universality for the preparation of MOF@GOx $@$ BHb-MIPs with effective selectivity using diverse templates, which provided an effective method to achieve the specific detection of certain monosaccharides.
## 4. Conclusion
Herein, porous MOF was prepared as the host matrix for the immo­ bilization of both GOx and BHb, obtaining a cascaded catalytic system $(\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{BHb})$ with both enhanced GOx activity and peroxidase-like activity. Then, using $\upbeta$ -D-glucose as both template molecule and substrate, hydroxyl radicals can be produced continuously and applied for the preparation of MIPs on the surface of $\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{GOx}@\operatorname{BHb}$ . Impressively, the obtained MOF $@$ GOx@BHb-MIPs could achieve the sensitive and specific detection of $\upbeta$ -D-glucose by colorimetry. Similarly, MOF@GOx@BHbMIPs-x, $\mathsf{M O F@G O x@B H b–M I P s–m}$ and $\mathrm{MOF@GOx@BHb–MIPs-g}$ were subsequently obtained using xylose, mannose and galactose as template molecules, respectively, and showed satisfied specific catalytic activity towards corresponding templates, indicating the effectiveness of the proposed strategy to achieve the highly specific catalytic analysis using GOx. It could be expected that the as-prepared biosensors will play an extremely important role in food safety such as accurate beverage composition analysis. All in all, the cascaded catalytic system for hydroxyl radical generation in a mild condition provides a powerful platform for preparation of MIPs in the presence of biological probes such as enzymes, which is of great significance for enhancing their specificity especially in sensing conditions containing multi-substrates.
